The Problem Statement
In the rapidly evolving landscape of modern logistics, the "last-mile" delivery phase remains the most inefficient 
and cost-intensive segment of the supply chain. Traditional delivery models are heavily reliant on human labor,
leading to significant bottlenecks caused by traffic congestion, high operational overhead, and carbon emissions. 
Furthermore, in specialized environments like university campuses, hospitals, or large-scale exhibitions, the need for a precise,
contact-less, and localized transport system is often unmet. 
Existing manual solutions fail to scale effectively and are prone to delays, creating a critical demand for a sustainable,
automated alternative that can navigate dynamic indoor and outdoor environments with minimal intervention.

The Solution Explanation
To address these challenges, I developed an Autonomous Delivery Device that integrates cutting-edge 
IoT architecture with robust Artificial Intelligence and navigation algorithms. 
The system leverages a suite of ultrasonic and infrared sensors managed by a centralized microcontroller to 
facilitate real-time obstacle detection and pathfinding. By applying advanced Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA),
the device calculates the most energy-efficient routes, ensuring high-speed data processing and reliable movement.

The software stack, primarily built using Python and C++, incorporates Computer Vision for environmental mapping and 
Embedded Systems for precise motor control. This synergy allows the device to operate autonomously in crowded 
spaces—as successfully demonstrated during its exhibition at the Kolkata Book Fair. 
This solution not only reduces human error and logistics costs but also aligns with global sustainability 
goals by utilizing an all-electric, low-energy consumption framework. By bridging the gap between digital command and physical delivery, 
this project serves as a scalable prototype for the future of smart, autonomous urban infrastructure.
